WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.212 --> 00:00:03.000
(pensive music)

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:04.470
- [Narrator] These are some of the scenes

00:00:04.470 --> 00:00:06.450
in the Reykjanes Peninsula of Iceland

00:00:06.450 --> 00:00:08.850
after a volcanic eruption began expelling

00:00:08.850 --> 00:00:10.890
lava in the region.

00:00:10.890 --> 00:00:14.220
- This style of eruption
differs somewhat from the kind

00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:17.837
of popular culture explosions
that you might see.

00:00:17.837 --> 00:00:21.432
(explosions blasting)

00:00:21.432 --> 00:00:23.370
(air whooshing)

00:00:23.370 --> 00:00:25.590
- [Narrator] Here's why this
eruption looks so different

00:00:25.590 --> 00:00:27.273
and what threats it poses.

00:00:28.470 --> 00:00:31.560
Iceland is home to more than 130 volcanoes

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:34.620
across more than 30 volcanic systems,

00:00:34.620 --> 00:00:36.540
and right now, lava is erupting

00:00:36.540 --> 00:00:39.030
out of the Svartsengi volcanic system

00:00:39.030 --> 00:00:42.630
located in the Reykjanes
Peninsula of southwest Iceland.

00:00:42.630 --> 00:00:46.650
- In a traditional volcano,
the volcanic material funnels

00:00:46.650 --> 00:00:50.220
through a central vent,
typically, like in those volcanoes

00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:54.477
that you ideate in Hollywood
where there is a mountain,

00:00:54.477 --> 00:00:56.400
and a conical peak, and the summit,

00:00:56.400 --> 00:00:59.040
and everything, like,
flows out of the summit,

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:00.300
whether that's an ash plume

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:03.000
that explodes explosively into the sky,

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:05.070
or whether the lava comes
up and out of the center

00:01:05.070 --> 00:01:07.080
and, like, leaks down
the side of the mountain.

00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:08.730
Here, it's not like that.

00:01:08.730 --> 00:01:10.740
- [Narrator] Instead, in
this type of eruption,

00:01:10.740 --> 00:01:13.800
known as a fissure eruption,
lava basically oozes

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:16.020
from cracks in the ground,

00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:18.840
and the type of lava typically
involved in these two kinds

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:21.270
of eruptions also looks different.

00:01:21.270 --> 00:01:24.990
An explosive eruption is often
due to viscous, sticky magma

00:01:24.990 --> 00:01:28.410
that doesn't flow easily,
while a fissure eruption pushes

00:01:28.410 --> 00:01:32.550
out fluid and runny magma that
spreads across the landscape.

00:01:32.550 --> 00:01:33.960
- The big danger with these is

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:37.410
that these lava flows can
spread out quite a long way,

00:01:37.410 --> 00:01:40.470
and obviously, as we're
seeing in Iceland right now,

00:01:40.470 --> 00:01:43.650
that poses a threat to
both the infrastructure

00:01:43.650 --> 00:01:45.303
in the region and people's lives.

00:01:46.860 --> 00:01:47.693
- [Narrator] The eruption

00:01:47.693 --> 00:01:50.850
from the Svartsengi volcanic
system occurred near Grindavik,

00:01:50.850 --> 00:01:52.530
an Icelandic fishing town.

00:01:52.530 --> 00:01:55.350
The surrounding area is
home to a major airport,

00:01:55.350 --> 00:01:58.110
one of the country's most
popular tourist attractions,

00:01:58.110 --> 00:02:01.410
the Blue Lagoon, and a power
plant that provides hot water

00:02:01.410 --> 00:02:03.273
for the majority of the peninsula.

00:02:05.550 --> 00:02:08.130
Eruptions in this part of
Iceland have been increasing

00:02:08.130 --> 00:02:12.180
steadily in recent years after
a very long dormant period.

00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:15.120
- This is just an area
of Iceland that goes

00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:19.740
through these periodic eras of
on-and-off eruption activity

00:02:19.740 --> 00:02:23.370
and then quiescence, so, like,
around a millennia or so,

00:02:23.370 --> 00:02:24.660
the peninsula is quiet.

00:02:24.660 --> 00:02:28.318
You don't see volcanic
activity, and then it starts up.

00:02:28.318 --> 00:02:31.260
(lava roaring)

00:02:31.260 --> 00:02:36.240
And then that era of volcanic
activity lasts for centuries.

00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:38.280
- [Narrator] Iceland sits
on top of the Eurasian

00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:41.460
and North American tectonic
plates, making it a hotbed

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:44.370
of seismic and volcanic activity.

00:02:44.370 --> 00:02:46.770
When the tectonic plates
slip past one another,

00:02:46.770 --> 00:02:49.620
earthquakes occur, and as
the plates spread apart,

00:02:49.620 --> 00:02:51.660
magma can leak up to the surface,

00:02:51.660 --> 00:02:53.850
generating volcanic eruptions.

00:02:53.850 --> 00:02:56.040
- The focus of sort of monitoring efforts

00:02:56.040 --> 00:03:00.450
of volcanic systems is around
trying to identify as early

00:03:00.450 --> 00:03:04.710
as possible any precursory
signals that sort of come

00:03:04.710 --> 00:03:07.050
before the onset of an eruption.

00:03:07.050 --> 00:03:10.590
And in instances like what
we're seeing in Iceland,

00:03:10.590 --> 00:03:13.320
these eruptions are typically
heralded by these sort

00:03:13.320 --> 00:03:17.820
of really high levels of seismic activity

00:03:17.820 --> 00:03:22.230
to try and really tell us where
there might be an eruption

00:03:22.230 --> 00:03:25.293
and sort of where ongoing
intrusions are going.

00:03:26.220 --> 00:03:28.740
- [Narrator] It remains to
be seen how the lava erupting

00:03:28.740 --> 00:03:32.040
from the Svartsengi volcanic
system will impact residents

00:03:32.040 --> 00:03:33.660
in the surrounding area.

00:03:33.660 --> 00:03:36.210
But the risk of volcanic
activity also comes

00:03:36.210 --> 00:03:38.940
with benefits for the Icelandic community.

00:03:38.940 --> 00:03:42.240
- Every expert from Iceland
who I spoke with was very proud

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:45.960
of the fact that the
country uses their volcanoes

00:03:45.960 --> 00:03:48.240
in a constructive way.

00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:50.610
They harness geothermal energy from them

00:03:50.610 --> 00:03:53.130
to generate electricity,
and heat their water,

00:03:53.130 --> 00:03:57.060
and heat their homes, and
they don't need to buy oil

00:03:57.060 --> 00:03:59.370
to do those things
because they're exploiting

00:03:59.370 --> 00:04:00.720
their volcanoes, basically.

00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:02.850
But of course, the trade off there is,

00:04:02.850 --> 00:04:05.460
in order to exploit them best,
you have to be close to them,

00:04:05.460 --> 00:04:07.260
and if you're close to them, eventually,

00:04:07.260 --> 00:04:10.530
an eruption will threaten
homes, threaten towns

00:04:10.530 --> 00:04:13.536
and cities, and with risks come benefits.

00:04:13.536 --> 00:04:16.203
(pensive music)

